.page chmod 1 change file permissions

# SYNOPSIS
*chmod* [*-R*] _MODE_ _FILE_...

# DESCRIPTION
`chmod` changes the permission bits of each _FILE_. _MODE_ is either an octal number (`644`) or a comma separated list of symbolic changes of the form _WHO_ _OP_ _PERMS_:

    WHO     u (owner), g (group), o (others), a (all); empty means all
    OP      + add the permissions, - remove them, = set exactly these
    PERMS   r (read), w (write), x (execute)

For example `u+x`, `go-w`, `a=r` or `u=rwx,go=rx`. The letters `s` and `t` are accepted but set no bits. Only the owner of a file, or root, may change its permissions.

# OPTIONS
@ *-R*
    Accepted, but directories are not descended into; combine `chmod` with find(1) for that.

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if a file or mode was bad.

# EXAMPLES
    chmod 600 secret.txt
    chmod +x script.sh
    chmod u=rw,go= private.txt
    find . -type f -exec chmod 644 {} +

# SEE ALSO
chown(1), ls(1), stat(1), find(1)
